/* ============================================================================
   expected-value-dice.epher -- the mean of one die and of two dice

   The expected value of a random outcome is the sum of x * P(X = x)
   over all outcomes x (Ross, "A First Course in Probability", ch. 4).
   For a fair die every face has probability 1/6, so

     E[X] = (1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6) / 6 = 21/6 = 3.5,

   and for the sum of two dice the definition sums over all 36 ordered
   pairs (a, b), each with probability 1/36. The sums below are exact:
   the packed while loops (guide 1.30) add exact rationals, and the
   totals come out as 3.5 and 7.

   Linearity gives the two-dice answer in one line: E[A + B] = E[A] +
   E[B] = 3.5 + 3.5 = 7, which the 36-term sum confirms.

   Source: Ross, "A First Course in Probability", chapter 4.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/probability/distributions/expected-value-dice.epher"
   Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- results ----
// One die, from the definition: sum x * 1/6 over the six faces.
v = {1, 0}
while v[1] <= 6 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + v[1] / 6}
print("E[one die] from the definition:", v[2])
print("as a fraction: 21/6 =", 21 / 6)
print("the arithmetic-mean formula (1 + 6) / 2:", (1 + 6) / 2)

// Two dice, from the definition over the 36 ordered pairs (a, b): the
// i-th pair has faces ceil(i / 6) and mod(i - 1, 6) + 1.
w = {1, 0}
while w[1] <= 36 do w = {w[1] + 1, w[2] + (ceil(w[1] / 6) + mod(w[1] - 1, 6) + 1) / 36}
print("E[sum of two dice] from the definition:", w[2])

// Linearity does the same sum in one step.
print("linearity 3.5 + 3.5:", 3.5 + 3.5)
/* ---- expected output ----
= {1, 0}
= E[one die] from the definition: 3.5
= as a fraction: 21/6 = 3.5
= the arithmetic-mean formula (1 + 6) / 2: 3.5
= {1, 0}
= E[sum of two dice] from the definition: 7
= linearity 3.5 + 3.5: 7
*/
